    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Society exhibits an obsession with gender in early childhood that creates the binary gender landscape\r\nwithin which a child lives. Previous research on gender assignment fails to consider the normative\r\nexpectations that lead a parent to ascribe socially constructed gender roles onto their children. This\r\nstudy substantiates the necessity to recognize gender assignment as an ongoing, socially motivated\r\nprocess that occurs before birth, at birth, and in early childhood. Interview and survey research was\r\nemployed to A) investigate parenting decisions and motivations to assign gender and B) decipher the\r\nmeasurable components of a social norm in gender assignment. Parents show evidence of mutually held\r\nbeliefs of social expectations to assign gender and indicate that these social expectations motivate their\r\ndecision to assign gender. These findings support the diagnosis of gender assignment as a social norm,\r\nenhance knowledge of gender assignment, and prompt change in parenting practices.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Babbit, L., Rosenfeld, M., and Stanford University Sociology Department  (2024). Gender in the Cradle: Evaluating Gender Assignment as a Social Norm. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/fp469ff4166.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
